{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CevKsEq3qgjcRs8WecaS6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now I have a complete picture of the codebase.\n\n---\n\n<<PHASE:DESIGN>>\n\n**Proposed change, file by file:**\n\n1. **Order.kt** — Add an `OrderStatus` enum with `ACTIVE` and `CANCELLED` values. Add a `status` field to the `Order` data class with a default of `ACTIVE` to preserve backward compatibility for creation.\n\n2. **OrderRepository.kt** — No changes needed; the in-memory storage is flexible enough to handle the status field.\n\n3. **OrderController.kt** — Add a `@PostMapping(\"/{orderId}/cancel\")` endpoint that:\n   - Finds the order; throws 404 if not found\n   - If already `CANCELLED`, returns 200 with the order (idempotent)\n   - Fetches all shipments for this order from the shipment repository\n   - If any shipment is `CONFIRMED`, throw 409 and change nothing\n   - Otherwise, update the order to `CANCELLED` and update all `CREATED` shipments to `CANCELLED`\n   - Return 200 with the updated order\n\n4. **ShipmentRepository.kt** — Add `findByOrderId(orderId: String): List<Shipment>` to find all shipments for an order.\n\n5. **ShipmentController.kt** — Add validation in the `create` method to reject shipment creation if the order exists but is `CANCELLED`, throwing a 409 `ConflictException`.\n\n6. **ApiError.kt** — Add `ORDER_CANCELLED` error code to the `ErrorCode` enum.\n\n7. **OrderControllerTest.kt** — Add tests covering:\n   - Cancel an `ACTIVE` order with `CREATED` shipments\n   - Idempotent cancel (cancel twice succeeds)\n   - Cancel fails with 409 if any shipment is `CONFIRMED` (and nothing changes)\n   - Cancel fails with 404 if order doesn't exist\n   - GET /orders/{orderId} returns the order with its status\n\n**Alternative considered and rejected:**\n- Using a service layer (`OrderService`) to encapsulate the cancellation logic: Rejected because the existing code doesn't use a service layer and the requirement is to preserve the existing architecture. A service would add complexity without being required by the ticket.\n- Using database transactions for atomicity: Rejected because this is in-memory storage with no transaction framework, and the existing code has no transaction handling. The ticket doesn't require a distributed transaction coordinator.\n- Recording shipment cancellation separately: Rejected because we can update the shipment status in-place in the in-memory store.\n\n**Data and error flow:**\n\n*Cancelling an ACTIVE order with CREATED shipments:*\n- Read: Find order by ID in OrderRepository\n- Validation: Check if any shipment has status CONFIRMED\n- Write: Update order status to CANCELLED, update all CREATED shipments to CANCELLED\n- Response: HTTP 200 with the updated order\n\n*Retrying a cancel (idempotent):*\n- Read: Find order by ID\n- Check: Order is already CANCELLED\n- Response: HTTP 200 with the order (no changes)\n\n*Cancel blocked by CONFIRMED shipment:*\n- Read: Find order and all its shipments\n- Validation: Find CONFIRMED shipment; abort here\n- Response: HTTP 409 with error code `ORDER_CANCELLED` (using this to signal the order cannot be cancelled due to its state)\n- No writes occur\n\n*Cancelling a non-existent order:*\n- Read: Find order by ID; result is null\n- Response: HTTP 404 with error code `ORDER_NOT_FOUND`\n\n*Creating a shipment for a CANCELLED order:*\n- Read: Find order by ID\n- Validation: Order exists and status is CANCELLED\n- Response: HTTP 409 with error code `ORDER_CANCELLED`\n- No write occurs\n\n**Test strategy:**\n\n1. **Test: Cancel ACTIVE order returns 200 and cancels CREATED shipments** — Create an order, create shipments in CREATED status, cancel the order, verify it's CANCELLED and shipments are CANCELLED.\n\n2. **Test: Idempotent cancel succeeds** — Create order, cancel it, cancel it again, verify 200 and no errors.\n\n3. **Test: Cancel fails with 409 if CONFIRMED shipment exists** — Create order, create shipments with one CONFIRMED (manually set via repository), attempt cancel, verify 409 and that order stays ACTIVE and shipments unchanged.\n\n4. **Test: Cancel returns 404 for unknown order** — Attempt cancel on non-existent order, verify 404 with ORDER_NOT_FOUND.\n\n5. **Test: GET /orders/{orderId} returns status** — Create order, verify it has status ACTIVE; cancel it, verify it has status CANCELLED.\n\n6. **Test: Cannot create shipment for CANCELLED order** — Create order, cancel it, attempt to create shipment, verify 409 with ORDER_CANCELLED.\n\n---\n\n<<PHASE:IMPLEMENTATION>>\n\nNow I'll implement the changes:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1122,"cache_read_input_tokens":19562,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1122},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21e5bb3a-642c-40bb-b375-508dd11be121","uuid":"0503e2a8-e842-4ea5-a2f6-d0f74e2912c5","timestamp":"2026-09-10T19:40:02.481Z","request_id":"req_011CevKsDzgom3ZiHaUwApPo"}
